      "latency_ms": 30908.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1155,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. Chemical Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the electrons in the bond spend more time near the oxygen atom. This gives the oxygen end of the molecule a slight negative charge and the hydrogen ends a slight positive charge. This polarity is what allows water to stick to itself and dissolve other substances.\n\n### 2. Physical States\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists as a solid, liquid, and gas at temperatures commonly found on the planet.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure that is less dense than liquid water (which is why ice floats).\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we interact with most. It is fluid, flows, and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air as vapor.\n\n### 3. The \"Universal Solvent\"\nWater is excellent at dissolving other substances. Because of its polar nature, it can pull apart the molecules of many other compounds. This is why it is called the \"universal solvent.\" It is the primary way nutrients and minerals are transported through plants and animals.\n\n### 4. High Specific Heat\nWater can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This property helps regulate the Earth's climate (oceans absorb heat from the sun) and keeps human bodies from overheating when we sweat.\n\n### 5. Essential for Life\nWater is the foundation of life as we know it. It is a major component of all living things (the human body is about 60% water) and is necessary for:\n*   Chemical reactions in cells.\n*   Transporting nutrients and waste.\n*   Lubricating joints.\n*   Regulating body temperature."
